<Base title="Support: Crustulator" description="Answers to common Crustulator questions about yeast amounts, units, share links and saved recipes.">
  <article>
    <h1>Support</h1>
    <p>Stuck on a dough? Start with these answers.</p>

    <details>
      <summary>Why is the yeast amount an estimate?</summary>
      <p>
        Crustulator works out yeast from your schedule with a fermentation model. Flour, starters, water and kitchens all behave a little differently, so treat the number as a strong starting point and watch the dough. If it rises too fast next time, use a little less yeast or a cooler spot.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>What does hydration mean?</summary>
      <p>
        Hydration is the weight of the water as a percentage of the weight of the flour. A 62% dough has 620 g of water for every 1,000 g of flour. Salt, oil, sugar and yeast are measured the same way, which is why bakers call these baker’s percentages.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do I switch between grams and ounces?</summary>
      <p>
        Tap the gear on the Dough screen to open Settings. You can pick grams or ounces, Celsius or Fahrenheit and centimeters or inches separately.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a kitchen scale?</summary>
      <p>
        A scale makes a huge difference, especially for flour and water. For tiny amounts of salt and yeast Crustulator also shows teaspoon estimates. Settings lets you pick the kind of salt you have so the spoons come out right.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do share links work?</summary>
      <p>
        The recipe is packed into the link itself, so there is nothing to sign in to. Open a link on the web to see the dough in your browser or open it on an iPhone with Crustulator installed to save it to your recipes.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Where are my saved recipes?</summary>
      <p>
        Recipes are saved on the device you made them on. The iPhone app and the web app keep separate lists, so use a share link to move a dough from one to the other.
      </p>
    </details>

    <h2>Still stuck?</h2>
    <p>
      Open an issue on <a href="https://github.com/flavormingo/crustulator/issues">GitHub</a> and describe what you tried. Screenshots of your dough settings help a lot.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
